Original commit body from aa092a823bed738939f95da74304a7ee7e1a412e:

On add, a SimHash match tells you to run
  update <ID> --duplicate-of <ID>        (to mark as duplicate)
  update <ID> --not-duplicate-of <ID>    (to suppress)
Neither flag is real. `update` accepts --add-duplicate-of and
--add-not-duplicate-of, so following the warning exits with
`unrecognized arguments`. Both suggestions were wrong, not just one.

This warning is the only place most agents learn how to resolve a
near-duplicate, so a wrong flag name there is self-defeating: it converts the
one actionable instruction into a dead end. It had already been hit twice and
worked around rather than fixed -- two tracker discussions record the dead end
verbatim ("update --not-duplicate-of does not exist despite the warning naming
it, so this note is the record"). Two agents each wrote a note explaining why
they could not run the command, and moved on. That is a root cause surviving two
encounters because each one was cheaper to route around than to fix.

WHY THE EXISTING TEST DID NOT CATCH IT. test_similarity_warning_on_add asserts
`pytest.warns(UserWarning, match="similar")`. Matching one word of prose cannot
observe whether the flags the message names exist. The new guard extracts every
--flag token from the emitted warning and asserts each is a real option of the
LIVE `update` subparser, read off argparse rather than a hardcoded list -- so a
future rename of either flag fails here instead of in a user's terminal. Written
first and confirmed RED, naming both offenders:
  AssertionError: warning suggests flags `update` does not accept:
  ['--duplicate-of', '--not-duplicate-of']

SAME DEFECT IN THE ADR. ADR-0013 carried the identical two non-existent flags in
its illustrative warning block and in its command table, so an agent reading the
ADR got the same broken command. Both corrected. The illustration also promised
output the warning has never emitted (a `title overlap:` percentage and a
`tracker show` suggestion); it now matches the text actually produced. And the
paragraph above it claimed the store warns at 13 bits while validation uses 8 --
both are 8, pinned equal by test_simhash_threshold_is_unified_with_validation,
so the stale claim is corrected rather than left standing next to the edit.

Checked and NOT changed: validation.py's near-duplicate warning suggests no
flags at all, so it has nothing to get wrong. The only remaining references to
the bad flag names are in two historical .ops records, which correctly describe
what was true when they were written.

Verified: the suggested commands now parse against the live parser (both
--add-duplicate-of and --add-not-duplicate-of accepted); 2424 passed, 4 skipped,
mypy ratchet clean.

